{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.Archimedean.LobachevskyDerivative","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.Archimedean.LobachevskyDerivative","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic","lean:module:QOU.Archimedean.BlochWignerLobachevsky","lean:module:QOU.Knot.Clausen"],"declarations":8,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.deriv_deriv_Lob_pos","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.deriv_deriv_Lob_pos","kind":"theorem","line":155,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/LobachevskyDerivative.lean#L155-L164","doc":"**`Λ'' > 0` on `(π/2, π)`** — the companion to `deriv_deriv_Lob_neg`, making\nthe non-concavity of `Λ` on `(0, π)` explicit rather than implied. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.deriv_deriv_Lob_neg","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.deriv_deriv_Lob_neg","kind":"theorem","line":138,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/LobachevskyDerivative.lean#L138-L153","doc":"**`Λ` is strictly concave-ish pointwise: `Λ'' < 0` on `(0, π/2)`.**\n\n`−cot t < 0` exactly when `cos t > 0`, i.e. on `(0, π/2)`.  Recorded because it\nis the sign that drives the diagonal entries `f_αα = −(cot α + cot γ) < 0`; note\n`Λ''` is *positive* on `(π/2, π)`, so `Λ` alone is NOT concave on `(0, π)` —\nconcavity of the tetrahedron volume comes from the SUM, via\n`cot_add_cot_pos`, not from `Λ` termwise.  Stating this guards against the\nplausible-but-false reading that `Λ` is concave. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.deriv_deriv_Lob","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.deriv_deriv_Lob","kind":"theorem","line":133,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/LobachevskyDerivative.lean#L133-L136","doc":"`Λ''(t) = −cot t`, in `deriv`-of-`deriv` form. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.hasDerivAt_deriv_Lob","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.hasDerivAt_deriv_Lob","kind":"theorem","line":112,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/LobachevskyDerivative.lean#L112-L131","doc":"**`Λ''(t) = −cot t` on `(0, π)`.**\n\nThis is the identity `IdealTetrahedronConcavity` states in prose and uses to\nwrite the ideal-tetrahedron Hessian\n\n    f_αα = −(cot α + cot γ),  f_αβ = −cot γ,  f_ββ = −(cot β + cot γ)\n\nfor `f(α,β) = Λ(α) + Λ(β) + Λ(π−α−β)`.  Proving it here is what makes that\nHessian the Hessian **of the Lobachevsky volume sum**, rather than of an\nunnamed function with the right second derivatives.\n\nStated via `EqOn` on the open interval so that `deriv (deriv Lob)` may be\nrewritten along it: `deriv Λ` agrees with `−log(2 sin ·)` throughout `(0, π)`,\nand differentiating that gives `−cot`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.hasDerivAt_neg_log_two_sin","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.hasDerivAt_neg_log_two_sin","kind":"theorem","line":95,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/LobachevskyDerivative.lean#L95-L110","doc":"**`(−log(2 sin ·))' = −cot`.**  The derivative of `Λ'`, stated on its own so\nthe second-derivative fact is available without unfolding `Λ`.\n\n`d/dt −log(2 sin t) = −(2 cos t)/(2 sin t) = −cos t / sin t`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.deriv_Lob","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.deriv_Lob","kind":"theorem","line":89,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/LobachevskyDerivative.lean#L89-L93","doc":"`deriv Λ t = −log(2 sin t)` on `(0, π)`, the `deriv`-form of\n`hasDerivAt_Lob`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.differentiableAt_Lob","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.differentiableAt_Lob","kind":"theorem","line":84,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/LobachevskyDerivative.lean#L84-L87","doc":"`Λ` is differentiable on `(0, π)`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.hasDerivAt_Lob","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.hasDerivAt_Lob","kind":"theorem","line":59,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/LobachevskyDerivative.lean#L59-L82","doc":"**`Λ'(t) = −log(2 sin t)` on `(0, π)`.**\n\nThe chain rule through `Λ(t) = Cl₂(2t)/2`: the inner derivative `2` cancels the\nouter `1/2`, and `Cl₂'(2t) = −log(2 sin(2t/2)) = −log(2 sin t)`.\n\nThis is the statement that `Λ` is the primitive `Λ(x) = −∫₀ˣ log|2 sin t| dt` in\nthe standard normalisation — the form in which the Lobachevsky function is\nusually defined, recovered here from the `Cl₂` series rather than assumed. ","signatureChunk":0}]}